Add browser-based VS Code workflow for rumpelpod

Add a VS Code extension for switching among pods with persistent agent
terminals and native review diffs. Include generated Rust-to-TypeScript
types, browser-mode development tooling, devcontainer services, and real
daemon browser integration coverage. Keep the browser server
unauthenticated and reachable only through loopback port forwarding.

Serve a separate anyhow demo repository so live test pods use a standard
nested runtime. Keep the demo beside the rumpelpod checkout under
`/workspaces` so it is easy to find from the development container.

Restore a Rumpelpod Activity Bar mode with agent sessions rendered by
extension-owned xterm.js terminals in the primary sidebar. Keep the
native review diff in the main editor and let VS Code choose inline or
side-by-side rendering. Preserve the compact pod switcher, creation and
shell controls, and event-driven status and review updates.

Keep pod selection available when another pod has a stale Git callback.
Only explicit refresh actions synchronize refs; picker and restoration
reads use the daemon's current inventory.

Always open a native review editor when selecting or restoring a pod.
Show an empty changes editor for clean pods instead of leaving the main
editor blank or displaying the previous pod's diff.

Make the active pod name the sidebar's pod selector and put its agent
and shell sessions in extension-owned xterm tabs that keep their PTYs
alive. Show every changed file in one native vertically stacked VS Code
review with concise pod and file-path labels. Keep terminal focus intact
when daemon events refresh that review.

Keep reviews unpinned and closable, but out of preview mode so opening a
different pod does not replace them. Keep background refreshes from
stealing another editor's focus or resurrecting a closed review, and
expose a View diff action that focuses or reopens it.

When selecting or creating a pod, focus its existing review or open a
new one. Leave unrelated pod reviews open and avoid duplicate tabs when
returning to a pod.

Separate workspace-wide pod creation into VS Code's native view toolbar.
Add merge, stop, delete, and SSH key actions for the selected pod, while
keeping destructive and secondary operations in a grouped overflow menu.
Remove the redundant global session restart action.

Force SSH key loading through the extension askpass helper so encrypted
keys prompt in VS Code instead of reading the extension host terminal.

Clear the active pod after merge, stop, or delete and close its review.

Keep VS Code's normal editor tabs and move pod, agent, and creation
selectors into popovers anchored to the Rumpelpod sidebar header. Hide
the terminal session tabs until more than one session is open.

Launch multiple agent sessions concurrently in the same pod. Make
header popovers toggle from their trigger and show the complete pod list
without a filter field.

Keep the existing terminal spacing and scrollbar behavior unchanged.
Theme only xterm's black fallback viewport to match the VS Code sidebar.

Start the baked development daemon socket and browser VS Code service
from a lingering systemd user session during container boot. Keep
startup independent of devcontainer lifecycle commands so rumpel-created
pods expose their editor without a background post-create build race.

Remove the extension's native node-pty dependency. Prepare agent and
shell sessions with a short-lived rumpel command, then connect xterm.js
directly to the daemon's Unix-socket WebSocket terminal protocol. Let
the daemon own Codex PTYs and relay pod-hosted agent and shell sessions,
including input, resize, detach, reconnect, and shell termination.

Build one platform-independent VSIX and attach it directly to tagged
GitHub releases. Publish the platform binaries separately as well, so
the installer downloads only the binary needed for the current Linux or
macOS host instead of the aggregate release archive. Document
installing the VSIX without a marketplace and using it with the Linux
daemon in WSL.
